#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""glaeda-disk: say where the disk went, and reclaim what is safe to rebuild.

    glaeda-disk report              report: biggest items, guards, reclaimable total
    glaeda-disk keepup --apply      pressure/idle cleanup using the selected role
    glaeda-disk                     report: biggest items, verdicts, reclaimable total
    glaeda-disk --apply             delete every reclaimable item, largest first
    glaeda-disk --pressure --apply  act only under disk pressure, stop at the free target
    glaeda-disk --idle --apply      while no job runs, delete what sat idle past its family's age
    glaeda-disk --pressure --idle --apply  pressure first; with no pressure, an hourly idle sweep
    glaeda-disk --low 15% --target 25%  thresholds as GiB free or as a share of each filesystem
    glaeda-disk --pressure --apply waits while a GitHub Actions job runs, unless below --emergency
    glaeda-disk --refresh           re-measure every size now instead of using the snapshot
    glaeda-disk --dedupe            store identical Xcode package/module files once (APFS clones), in
                                    Xcode's DerivedData and the cmux-next workflow's per-runner copies
                                    and, at a 16 KiB floor, the capture and evidence trees that
                                    disk-policy.json's dedupe_capture_roots names
    glaeda-disk --dedupe --dedupe-builds
                                    additionally clone identical files in inactive cmux-next Products trees
    glaeda-disk --runner-caches     use only the enrolled runner-account cache allowlist

Parallel sessions each keep a full DerivedData, and its SourcePackages artifacts and module
caches are byte-identical across them. --dedupe replaces each duplicate with a copy-on-write
clone of one canonical file, keeping the duplicate's mode and timestamps. Builds see the same
bytes; the disk stores them once. du still counts clones in full; df shows the real saving.

macOS keeps no directory sizes (Spotlight has none for folders), so measuring
means walking every file, which is disk-latency bound. Sizes are therefore kept
in a snapshot: a report prints instantly from it, re-judges every verdict live
(processes, idle time, git), sizes only new items, and re-measures in the
background once the snapshot is older than --max-age minutes.

Only families whose contents are reproducible build output or scratch space are
ever deleted. Everything else is reported so the space is at least visible.
An item is never deleted while a process has its cwd inside it, a process command
line names it, it holds a git checkout, or it changed within the idle window.
A cache is recognized by its content, never its name (cache_layout: a CACHEDIR.TAG,
npm's cacache, a hex-sharded content store like Go's build cache, bun's install cache,
a rustup toolchain, Go's module cache, a Chromium disk cache, a Bazel output root).
In the cache families (not tmp or scratch, whose checkouts are judged in full) such a
tree is "sealed": the git search checks its top two levels, where a person's clone would
sit, and does not walk the millions of blobs below. Any other tree is searched in full,
and one too large to finish, unreadable, or holding another filesystem stays unchecked (kept).
The home-caches family finds caches anywhere in the home directory by the layouts that name
the tool refilling them (DISCOVERABLE: ~/.npm/_cacache, ~/.bun/install/cache, a cargo-tagged
~/.cargo/registry, ~/go/pkg/mod, a rustup toolchain nothing pins), never a Python environment
(uv and direnv tag those too). Least recently used goes first, counting file access where the
filesystem records it; a cache in use is kept by process evidence. Secrets, keychains,
checkouts, other filesystems, the Chromium trees and the macOS privacy-protected folders are
never searched (HOME_NEVER).
Bazel's macOS output root (/private/var/tmp/_bazel_$USER) is its own family.
Every deletion is appended to a JSONL receipt.
An apply also appends one bounded eviction summary with before/after ``df`` free
blocks, logical catalogue bytes, blocked/unchecked bytes, and the accounting gap;
those quantities are intentionally kept separate because APFS clones and concurrent
jobs make logical deletion sizes an unreliable physical-space measurement.

The cmux-next workflow keeps per-runner state under
~/Library/Caches/cmux-next-ci.  Its SourcePackages and module/SDK caches are
reusable inputs, so the explicit cmux-next family below only considers the
generated Build, Index, Logs and Products children beneath a validated runner
or dev-daily Release root.  Those children require a DEFAULT_AGE_HOURS (12 h) idle
window plus a fresh process/lock/lease check; unknown children and all preserved
inputs remain visible to accounting and are kept.  The cache has an LRU size cap
(cmux_next_ci_cap_gib in disk-policy.json or --cmux-next-cap-gib, default 40 GiB, 0 off): while a
pressure run measures it over the cap, the window gives way, least recently used first, until it is
under the cap or the free target is met, and a state root idle past a job's timeout goes whole, inputs
included, whoever owns it; a younger state loses only generated output. The process/lock/lease vetoes still
hold, and a runner's running job keeps the states it has built in since it began (a state last built in before
that is its last job's, as an idle runner's is) (docs/CMUX_NEXT_CI_RETENTION.md). Only a pressure run walks the cache; a text
report shows its last measurement, and its run line records how far over the cap it is.
The per-host build root (cmux_next_ci_host_roots, default dev-daily-release/host: one tree a mini's DEV
compiles share under flock on its lock, so their compilation cache hits) is never the cap's: it goes whole, after
everything else, only while free is still under the runner floor (exempt from age by role: the newest warm cache),
and never while its lock is held. Per-instance dev-daily-release states, now its fallback only, go first.

Each enrolled GitHub runner's checkout (~/actions-runner*/_work/<repo>/<repo>) has two families.
runner-checkout-build is SwiftPM's Packages/*/.build and Packages/*/*/.build plus cargo-tagged target
directories (cmux-tui/target), plus DerivedData a workflow keeps in $RUNNER_WORKSPACE/.glaeda-state
(_work/<repo>/.glaeda-state/*derived-data*); it goes only under pressure, only while that runner has no Runner.Worker,
an hour idle, least recently modified first. runner-git-garbage is git's tmp_pack_*/tmp_obj_* temp files an
interrupted fetch left; one an hour old goes whenever nothing holds it open. The checkout itself stays
until no job used it for 12 hours (any pressure or sweep pass then removes it), except as the last resort of a
pressure run still under the runner gate's disk floor after everything else (runner-idle-checkout): then the whole checkout of a runner with no Runner.Worker, unused for 10 minutes and
held open by nothing, goes, least recently used first, until free space reaches the floor plus
target_margin_gib. The runner's next job clones it again. Before that, on every pressure or sweep pass of a
fleet mini, runner-checkout-repack folds a checkout's packs with `git repack -a -d -k -l` (keeps every object) once
it has 40 or more, while its runner has no Runner.Worker and its git dir was quiet for 10 minutes: jobs fetch
into it with gc.auto=0 (cmux's nightly.yml writes it), so git never consolidates them itself.

Tests leave one small scratch entry per case in the runner user's $TMPDIR (cmux-cli-socket-<uuid>,
git-pr-<uuid>, TemporaryDirectory.<rand>, ...): tens of thousands, each under the 50 MiB item floor, so no
family reclaims them. user-tmp-test-residue removes entries named by TMP_TEST_RESIDUE on every apply (not only
under pressure) once nothing in the whole entry changed for 12 hours and no process holds or names it. On a fleet
mini with enrolled runners it also takes test fixture names: dash-separated words, a full UUID and at most a short
extension (TMP_TEST_UUID_NAME), never a dotted or bare-UUID name, a TMP_TEST_RESIDUE_KEEP name (com.apple.*, dot
names, tmux/ssh, signing, keychain, credential, export, release...) or a credential extension; such an entry also
stays while a socket sits in it or is one. Any other name stays: age alone does not prove an entry disposable.
A measured-prefix entry with a TemporaryItems child (macOS guards $TMPDIR/<entry>/TemporaryItems) is moved into a
holding dir one level down (glaeda-tmp-hold-<pid>, itself named scratch with the socket veto if a pass dies),
walked and judged there, and deleted there or moved back. Every report shows the count
($TMPDIR entries, test scratch, untouched 12 h+; JSON tmp_test_residue) from one listing, no walk or du.

A clone directly in a CI runner account's home (~/cmux) that is fetched branch after branch gains a pack per
fetch and git consolidates only at 50: home-clone-gc runs `git gc --prune=1.day.ago` (work trees never pruned)
on the clone with the most packs once it has ten, at most one per idle sweep or pressure pass and after the
eviction lock is released, when no lock, missing work tree, process, command or hour-old git activity touches
it or its linked work trees. Only a home with enrolled runners. A slow gc runs on; it is never killed.

The runner-account profile is intentionally narrower than the normal user profile:
it covers only DerivedData, cmux reload/GhosttyKit caches, and CUA recordings/frame
caches, with a DEFAULT_AGE_HOURS (12 h) minimum idle age. Git index refreshes are ignored for that
age clock; process/open-file/command/socket/git-loss checks still veto deletion.

The idle sweep (--idle) reclaims whatever the free space. A busy host sweeps too: deletes skip only what a
job uses (open, named by a command, a root's current seed or checkout), not the whole pass. The old hold
(no sweep while a GitHub Actions job, build, dev-build worker job, reservation or held host lock) and the
pressure pass's defer-while-CI-runs come back with "defer_during_ci": true in ~/.config/glaeda/disk-policy.json.
Its windows follow reuse (IDLE_SWEEP_HOURS and friends). cmux CI's hot tier ages in host CI jobs since its
last use, not hours (item_due: pressure_jobs, min_jobs, sweep_jobs, soft_idle_hours in disk-policy.json). It also
reports Xcode copies no pin names and simulator runtimes, and deletes a runtime only when no device
uses it, it is not its platform's newest, it went unused for RUNTIME_IDLE_DAYS and no simulator is
booted (simctl runtime delete restarts CoreSimulatorService). Xcode copies are never deleted. A shutdown
simulator device last booted SIM_DEVICE_IDLE_HOURS ago is erased (`simctl erase`: its name, type and runtime stay,
its data goes), never deleted, unless a process names its UDID or has a file open in it.

On an owned cmux mini, cmux CI's warm state under /Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet/ci is a local hot
tier, evicted LRU under pressure: parked pull request builds past the measured host-job reuse distance, seeds other than each
root's newest one under pressure (two during idle sweeps), and a root's kept build once the root has been idle two days. On the
trusted seeder, a seed its serve log shows streamed whole to the seed archive (cmux-lawrence's SSD) is
reclaimable at once instead of after the idle window: the PR minis restore it from there over the LAN.
SwiftPM scratch (spm-scratch/<fingerprint>) that no job holds by its lock ages like any cache, and goes
whatever its age once free space is below the emergency floor. The Chromium source tree under
cmux-browser-fleet/build and protected release-support caches are never deleted. Small top-level
browser tour artifacts and fleet cache entries older than the 2026-09-17 migration cutoff are
disposable; newer ones stay when a live reservation or lease names them.

Under the runner gate's disk floor (FLOOR_HOLD) the windows that only keep things warm give way, least
recently used first and only down to the floor plus target_margin_gib: an idle runner's checkout build
output, its cmux-next scheme state's generated output (cmux-scheme/<runner name>, only while that runner
has no Runner.Worker), a canonical build root's admission DerivedData while no job holds the root's token,
and code-sign clones other than each bundle's newest copy and any running app's.
cmux7s sat at 21 GiB under a 30 GiB floor with 40 GiB of media CI's cmux DEV clones and 20 GiB of
per-runner cmux-next Build "recent" (2026-10-21). cmux14 sat at the floor with 17.9 GiB of admission
DerivedData idle 9-21 h on two roots no job held (2026-10-25).

Kill switch: while ~/.config/glaeda/disk-off exists, or disk-policy.json has "enabled": false, an
--apply (or --dedupe) run deletes nothing; it says so and exits 0. Every --apply run, whatever it
did, appends exactly one JSON line to the run log (macOS ~/Library/Logs/glaeda-disk-runs.jsonl, Linux
~/.local/state/glaeda/disk-runs.jsonl): free space before and after, target, floor, reclaimed, whether
the target was reached, the biggest blocked families, and why a run skipped (off, no pressure, a
lock held, a deferral).

Free space is judged per filesystem. On Linux /tmp is often its own tmpfs, so pressure there
reclaims only /tmp items, and pressure on the home filesystem never deletes from /tmp.
Thresholds scale with the disk: "15%:40-150" is 15% of it, at least 40 and at most 150 GiB,
so the same default fits a 256 GB laptop, a Mac mini and a multi-terabyte Mac Studio.

A tmpfs holds its files in RAM and swap, so its free space says nothing about pressure: a
16 GiB /tmp at 80% had filled swap while still "2.3G free". A tmpfs is judged by
--tmpfs-low/--tmpfs-target instead (default: pressure once a quarter of it is in use), and
its items count from 1 MiB.

The temp dir also counts top-level files (leaked extracted libraries, logs), and a scratch
checkout there is reclaimable when deleting it loses nothing
git holds: clean, no stash, no other worktrees, and every commit it points at (refs, HEAD,
reflogs) on a remote-tracking ref or confirmed by a remote server (`fetch --negotiate-only`:
no objects move, no refs change; unreachable means keep). Every submodule repository it would
take along, in its git dir or embedded in its tree, must pass the same test; pinned submodule
commits fetched by id are what the server confirms. A linked worktree must be unlocked, with
HEAD on a branch, tag or remote ref of its repository, which survives, and no commit only its
own reflog holds. Ignored files go with it: in a temp-dir checkout they are
build output or copies. Nested or unverifiable checkouts stay protected.

A mirror family's item (cmux-job-cache, the hq build cache: mirror_idle_hours) idle DEFAULT_AGE_HOURS goes
whole, but first archive_mirror writes every commit no remote holds, and every branch or tag only this host
has (what the servers confirm, never a local remote-tracking ref), into git bundles under ~/archive/glaeda-disk,
each checked with `git bundle verify`, never written over another, and listed with its sha256 in manifest.jsonl;
only listed bundles at that hash are pruned, MIRROR_ARCHIVE_DAYS later. A bundle that fails keeps the tree:
the receipt says blocked:bundle-failed, the run line lists it, and stderr alerts. Uncommitted or untracked work,
which no bundle holds, keeps it too (blocked:uncommitted); ignored build output goes. Right before the delete,
fresh process evidence and the repositories' refs as at bundle time are checked again.
"""

